      "body": "## Purpose\n\nFixes #63019.\n\n`DefaultModelLoader.counter_before_loading_weights` is only assigned inside `_get_weights_iterator()`, which runs when the generator returned by `get_all_weights()` is first pulled from. A model whose `load_weights()` never consumes that iterator — the reported case is a GGUF path that reads the checkpoint itself — leaves the counter at its `0.0` default, so the log line computes `time.perf_counter() - 0.0` and prints the raw monotonic clock, roughly the host's uptime:\n\n```\n(EngineCore pid=7234) INFO 10-22 01:46:51 [default_loader.py:501] Loading weights took 197518.83 seconds\n(EngineCore pid=7234) INFO 10-22 01:46:58 [model_runner.py:429] Model loading took 21.84 GiB memory and 23.915460 seconds\n```\n\nThe engine process had started 21 seconds earlier, so the first number is not a duration.\n\n## Change\n\nTake `load_weights_start = time.perf_counter()` on entry to `load_weights()`, and fall back to it when `counter_before_loading_weights` is still `0.0`.\n\nThe normal path is untouched: when the iterator is pulled from, `counter_before_loading_weights` is set there as before, so weight *download* time stays out of the reported duration. Only the case that previously printed the raw clock changes.\n\nThis follows the approach suggested in the issue.\n\n## Test Plan\n\nNo test is included, and I would rather say why than quietly omit it. The change affects one log line inside `load_weights()`, and exercising it would mean mocking most of that method — the quantization config, the EP and multimodal weight filters, `get_all_weights()`, and the strict weight-tracking check — for an assertion about a logged number. That seemed more likely to be brittle than useful.\n\nI also could not run the suite locally: I am on macOS without a CUDA toolchain, so I verified the change by reading the code path, plus `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` on the file. If you would like a test regardless, say which shape you prefer and I will add it.\n\n## Test Result\n\n`ruff check` and `ruff format --check` pass on the changed file. No behavioural change outside the log line.\n",
